<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-js"><code><span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight:=
 bold;">import</span> type { Tree } from <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">".=
/tree.ts"</span>;
<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">import</span> { leaf, no=
de, size } from <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"./tree.ts"</span>;

<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">const</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">t</span>: Tree&lt;string&gt; =3D node(leaf, <span styl=
e=3D"color: #ca3400;">"a"</span>, node(leaf, <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;=
">"b"</span>, leaf));
size(t); <span style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;">// </span><spa=
n style=3D"color: #8f6f4a; font-style: italic;">2</span>
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Use <code>From TypeScriptExtraction Require Import BigInt.</code> for exact=
 <code>bigint</code> arithmetic instead of <code>number</code>.
</p>
</div>
</li>
<li><a id=3D"org9f4145a"></a>Protecting boundaries with Zod<br />
<div class=3D"outline-text-5" id=3D"text-org9f4145a">
<p>
Because the generated types are ordinary TypeScript interfaces, you can pai=
r them with runtime validators such as Zod to enforce invariants at untrust=
ed boundaries (JSON, network, user input, etc.).
</p>

<p>
Rocq:
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-coq"><code><span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight=
: bold;">From</span> TypeScriptExtraction <span style=3D"color: #006f00; fo=
nt-weight: bold;">Require Import</span> Number.

<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">Record</span> <span styl=
e=3D"color: #a7601f;">point</span> :=3D { px : nat; py : nat }.

<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">Definition</span> <span =
style=3D"color: #a7601f;">swap</span> (<span style=3D"color: #007a9f;">p</s=
pan> : point) : point :=3D {| px :=3D py p; py :=3D px p |}.

TypeScript <span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">Extraction</s=
pan> <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"point.ts"</span> swap.
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Extracted TypeScript (<code>point.ts</code>):
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-js"><code><span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight:=
 bold;">export</span> <span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">in=
terface</span> Point {
  readonly px: number;
  readonly py: number;
}

<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">export</span> <span styl=
e=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">const</span> <span style=3D"color:=
 #007a9f;">swap</span> =3D (p: Point): Point =3D&gt; ({ px: p.py, py: p.px =
});
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
Usage with Zod:
</p>

<div class=3D"org-src-container">
<pre class=3D"src src-js"><code><span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight:=
 bold;">import</span> * as z from <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"zod"</sp=
an>;

<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">import</span> { swap } f=
rom <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"./point"</span>;
<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">import</span> type { Poi=
nt } from <span style=3D"color: #ca3400;">"./point"</span>;

<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">const</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">PointSchema</span> =3D z.toZod&lt;Point&gt;()(
  z
    .strictObject({
      px: z.number(),
      py: z.number(),
    })
    .readonly()
);

<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">const</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">untrustedJson</span> =3D {
  px: 100,
  py: 200,
};

<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">const</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">point</span> =3D PointSchema.parse(untrustedJson);
<span style=3D"color: #006f00; font-weight: bold;">const</span> <span style=
=3D"color: #007a9f;">result</span> =3D swap(point);

console.log(JSON.stringify(untrustedJson, <span style=3D"color: #00824f;">n=
ull</span>, 2));
console.log(JSON.stringify(result, <span style=3D"color: #00824f;">null</sp=
an>, 2));
</code></pre>
</div>

<p>
This keeps the trusted computational core (the extracted <code>swap</code>)=
 separate from boundary validation.
</p>

<p>
There is also a short explanatory video demonstrating the workflow (definin=
g and proving properties of a state machine in Rocq, extracting it, and usi=
ng the result directly with React=E2=80=99s <code>useReducer</code>) here:
</p>

<p>
<a href=3D"https://x.com/smorimoto/status/2118570731990876210">https://x.co=
m/smorimoto/status/2118570731990876210</a>
</p>
</div>
</li>
</ul>
</div>
